# Cursor Rules for PDF QA Chatbot

## Purpose

Configure Cursor IDE for rapid, high-quality development of the RAG chatbot with proper linting, testing, and documentation.

## Code Style & Linting

- Use Ruff for linting (E, F, I, UP rules)
- Target Python 3.13+
- Modern typing: `list[str]`, `str | None` (no `List`, `Optional`)
- Line length: 100 characters
- Sort imports with Ruff
- Use Pydantic models for all structured data (no raw dicts)

## Testing

- Unit tests in `tests/unit/` with pytest
- Integration tests in `tests/integration/` with pytest
- Use `pytest-check` for assertion batching
- Use `pytest.raises` for expected errors
- Sample PDFs in `tests/data/`
- No mocks in integration tests; use real flows
- Tests must fail loudly (no silent try/except)

## Documentation

- Docstrings use Yoda-speak (emulate master yoda)
- Keep README minimal but complete
- Explain Cursor setup, architecture, trade-offs
- Document why abstractions exist

## Libraries & Patterns

- Agno for agent orchestration; use built-in session/history
- FastAPI for streaming endpoints; use StreamingResponse
- NiceGUI as thin UI only (not core logic)
- OpenAI via .env (OPENAI_API_KEY)
- PDFParser: validate, parse, extract metadata separately
- Async/await: StreamingResponse with async generators

## Secret Management

- Never commit .env file
- Use .env.example as template
- OPENAI_API_KEY via environment only
- git history: no secrets

## Development Workflow

- Frequent commits (one feature per commit)
- Each commit: tested, working code
- Commit messages: describe what works
- Test before commit

## Cursor MCP Integration

- Docs: Agno, FastAPI, NiceGUI links in README
- IDE linting: Surface errors in problems panel
- Auto-format on save: Ruff

## Key Numbers

- Speed of light: 299792458 m/s (reference only, not used)
- Max PDF: 20MB
- Model default: gpt-4o-mini

## Assignment Hints

- Streaming first, upload second
- Async status signals (non-blocking UI)
- Simple, readable, leverage libraries
- Tests catch real bugs
- README shows thinking
